format 1
adapter curriculum 1
unit: radioactivity

objective u2-decay-changes-not-vanishes
statement: Know that a decay changes a nucleus into a different one; nothing simply vanishes.
misconception: Decay means the atom disappears.

item u2-l4-tom-different
type: true_or_myth
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-iaea-decay

statement:
    When a nucleus decays, it changes into a different nucleus.

answer: true

explanation:
    True. The parent changes into a daughter, which is a different nucleus.

item u2-l4-tom-vanish
type: true_or_myth
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-iaea-decay

statement:
    Radioactive decay means the atom just disappears.

answer: myth

explanation:
    Myth. The nucleus changes into a different nucleus. Nothing simply vanishes.

item u2-x-still-there
type: true_or_myth
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-iaea-decay

statement:
    After a decay there is still a nucleus. It is a different one.

answer: true

explanation:
    True. A decay changes a nucleus into a different nucleus.

item u2-x-what-happened
type: choice
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-iaea-decay

prompt:
    A nucleus decays. What has happened to it?

options:
    {"id":"changed","label":"It has changed into a different nucleus"}
    {"id":"vanished","label":"It has vanished","note":"Nothing simply vanishes."}
    {"id":"halved","label":"It has been cut in half","note":"A decay does not cut a nucleus in half."}

answer: changed

explanation:
    The nucleus changed into a different nucleus. Nothing simply vanishes.
